    "subtopic": "Right triangles & trigonometry",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "stem": "<p>In the triangle shown, what is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math>?</p>",
    "type": "grid-in",
    "correctAnswer": "45",
    "explanation": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math>. An isosceles right triangle has a right angle and two legs of equal length. In the triangle shown, one angle is a right angle and the two legs each have a length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"15\"><mn>15</mn></math>. Thus, the given triangle is an isosceles right triangle. In an isosceles right triangle, the measures of the two non-right angles are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45 degree\"><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. It follows that the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"45\"><mn>45</mn></math>.</p>",
